<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayinnaung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1516">1516</a>–<a href="/years/1581">1581</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Bayinnaung Kyawhtin Nawrahta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bayinnaung was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1550 to 1581, and created what may have been the largest empire in the history of South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. He reunited Burma after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a>, conquered the Shan states, took Chiang Mai in 1558 and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1564 and 1569, also bringing Lan Xang and Manipur under his overlordship. His empire, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>, broke up soon after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayinnaung.jpg" alt="Bayinnaung" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phyo WP · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayinnaung.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bayinnaung was born Ye Htut on 16 January 1516 to Mingyi Swe and Shin Myo Myat. His ancestry is unclear: no contemporary record mentions it, and the dynasty's chronicle first gave a genealogy in 1724. Both his parents joined the seven-person staff chosen in April 1516 to look after the royal baby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a>, and his mother was the prince's wet nurse. When Tabinshwehti became king in 1530 he married Ye Htut's sister and rewarded his childhood companions. Ye Htut won many key battles for him between 1534 and 1549, was made chief minister in 1539, and was Tabinshwehti's chosen heir apparent from 1542.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a> was assassinated by his own bodyguards on 30 April 1550, and the governors and viceroys, including Bayinnaung's brother, each proclaimed themselves king. Bayinnaung began his war of restoration at Toungoo. In 1552 he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> and defeated its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smim-htaw">Smim Htaw</a> in single combat on elephants. He was crowned on 12 January 1554 with the reign name Thiri Thudhamma Yaza. Ava fell on 22 January 1555 and he then conquered the Shan states between 1557 and 1563. Chiang Mai surrendered without a fight on 2 April 1558, and Manipur submitted around February 1560.</p><p>In 1563 he demanded a white elephant from the king of Siam, and when it was refused sent five armies of 60,000 men to Siam. He went on to put down revolts in Lan Na and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath">Setthathirath</a> of Lan Xang. His gains gave him at least nominal control from Manipur to Lan Xang. He rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> with 20 gates for his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states after a rebellion in 1565 burned the palace. He sent gifts to the Tooth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>, and in 1561 the Portuguese archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> destroyed the tooth relic that Bayinnaung had tried to buy; the Ceylonese held that it had been a copy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He made legal uniformity a part of his administration, standardised weights and measures, promoted orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> and forbade human and animal sacrifice. Otherwise he grafted the existing decentralised system onto a far larger realm, and his rule rested on personal allegiance. He died on 10 October 1581 after a long illness, leaving 97 children. Ava and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> revolted within just over two years and by 1599 all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states had declared independence. He is counted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a> as one of the three leading Burmese kings and is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> as the Conqueror of the Ten Directions, from a 1931 novel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bayinnaung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bayinnaung</b> forced Ayutthaya's submission in 1564 and took the city in 1569 after a long siege, making it a vassal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the sixteenth century its military strength faded as rival factions fought over the throne, and in 1558 the Burmese king <b>Bayinnaung</b> of the First Toungoo Empire captured Chiang Mai almost without resistance, beginning two centuries of Burmese domination.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Toungoo king <b>Bayinnaung</b> conquered Lan Na and Ayutthaya, Setthathirath (r. 1548–1571) allied with Ayutthaya, an alliance commemorated in the stupa of Phra That Si Song Rak, and moved his capital to Vientiane, further from the Burmese advance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bayinnaung</b> (r. 1550–1581) had built the largest empire in the history of mainland Southeast Asia, but under his son Nanda Bayin it disintegrated: Siam broke free, Lower Burma was depopulated by war and famine, and in 1599 the capital Pegu was sacked by the forces of Toungoo and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Having restored Tabinshwehti's empire, <b>Bayinnaung</b> was formally crowned in Pegu with the reign name Thiri Thudhamma Yaza.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-12">12 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>With starvation in the city and its allies from Ava and Mrauk U defeated by <b>Bayinnaung</b>, King Minkhaung of Prome surrendered and was taken to Toungoo with his queen.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-19">19 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Taungoo, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Bago, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.93,96.43&amp;year=1558" aria-label="Taungoo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7159 1462 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bayinnaung"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7609 1732h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7609 1732h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.93,96.43&amp;year=1558">What was here in 1558? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
